.TH TUBE 3
.SH NAME
tube \- tubes served by the kernel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind #= /mnt/tube

.B /mnt/tube/ctl
.BI /mnt/tube/ n
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The tube device connects the tubes of
.IR tsend (2)
with file descriptors and lets kernel processes use tubes and semaphores
of
.IR upsem (2)
shared with a user process.
It comes from the NIX kernel.
.PP
Each open of
.B ctl
makes a new file
.IR n ,
which the open file descriptor then refers to;
the directory lists the files in use.
Writing
.IP
.IB "fd inaddr outaddr"
.PP
to it, with the addresses of two tubes in the writer's memory
(either may be 0), starts kernel processes
.RB ( tin
and
.BR tout ,
with the writer's segments) that send each read of up to the
message size from the file descriptor
.I fd
through the tube at
.IR inaddr ,
padded with zeros, and write each message received from the tube at
.I outaddr
to
.IR fd .
Reading the file gives that line, or
.B none
before.
The processes stop when the last reference to the file is closed.
.PP
For tests, writing
.IP
.BI up " addr"
.br
.BI down " addr"
.PP
makes a kernel process do 10
.IR upsem s
(each after 300 ms) or
.IR downsem s
on the semaphore at
.IR addr ,
and
.IP
.BI send " addr"
.br
.BI recv " addr"
.PP
10
.IR tsend s
(each after 300 ms, the pointers 0x1000, 0x1001, ...) or
.IR trecv s
on the tube of pointers at
.IR addr .
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devtube.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/port/syssem.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tsend (2),
.IR upsem (2)
.SH BUGS
The device is only in the amd64 and arm64 kernels.
A tube's messages have a fixed size: a file descriptor's data is cut
into messages as its reads return it.
